WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:03.770
Imagine several colleagues who have stepped up to lead parts

2
00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:07.050
of an accessibility project, on top of their regular work.

3
00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:10.290
The project is going well. Then one of them tells

4
00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.430
her supervisor that she is stretched thin, and that her

5
00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.810
leadership work is not in anyone's job description.

6
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.900
That is such an honest moment, and I suspect it

7
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.480
is more common than most organizations realize. So what is

8
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.280
shared, or distributed, leadership?

9
00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:29.490
It treats leadership as something that moves across a team,

10
00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.910
based on who knows the most at each stage. A

11
00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.350
policy analyst might lead during drafting, a front line specialist

12
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:40.310
during community design, and a technology lead during rollout. The

13
00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:43.710
formal manager does not disappear. Their role shifts to building

14
00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:47.910
the network, resolving friction, protecting resources and keeping overall accountability.

15
00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.380
How is that different from participative leadership, where the leader

16
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.080
also invites a lot of input before making a decision?

17
00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:00.430
In participative leadership, one leader gathers input and still holds

18
00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.330
the final call. In shared leadership, actual ownership and authority

19
00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.690
move across the group. Influence flows to whoever has the

20
00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.510
most relevant knowledge for the problem at hand, regardless of

21
00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:10.710
rank.

22
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.520
What would I notice on a team that shares leadership

23
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.360
well? I imagine it could look a little chaotic from

24
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.140
the outside if it were not carefully organized.

25
00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:23.850
Different people chair meetings and represent the team, depending on

26
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.830
expertise. Colleagues coordinate and give feedback directly to one another.

27
00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:32.590
Budgets, priorities and risks are shared openly, so whoever leads

28
00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:36.470
has full context. Senior managers visibly defer to experts and

29
00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:39.830
credit them. And for each phase, everyone knows exactly who

30
00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:42.030
holds the lead and when it passes to someone else.

31
00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:46.720
Where is this approach most valuable? It sounds especially suited

32
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.140
to problems that cross many specialties at once.

33
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:54.810
Exactly. Cross agency work, integrated service networks and multi partner

34
00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:59.430
policy efforts span legal, clinical, technical and community knowledge, and

35
00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:03.030
no single executive holds all the answers. Shared leadership puts

36
00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.850
the right expert in front at the right moment.

37
00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:08.750
It also builds resilience: when a manager is away or

38
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.410
leaves, the team does not collapse, because many people have

39
00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:13.150
practiced leading.

40
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.880
And the risks? When everyone leads, it seems possible that

41
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.060
no one is really accountable.

42
00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:23.150
That is the main risk. Without clear decision rights for

43
00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.530
each phase, tasks slip, deadlines drift and meetings multiply. In

44
00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.250
a crisis, teams can lose time debating who should act,

45
00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.670
so an agreed incident lead is essential. And if formal

46
00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:37.330
leaders step back without clear rotation, the most assertive or

47
00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.730
well connected people quietly take over the shared roles.

48
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.760
The page describes real potential for equity here. What makes

49
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.960
shared leadership promising for fairness?

50
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:51.590
Senior ranks in many institutions are less diverse than the

51
00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:55.130
front line and the communities served. Sharing leadership lets diverse

52
00:02:55.130 --> 00:03:00.170
specialists, multilingual staff and disabled colleagues hold real influence now,

53
00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.810
rather than waiting years for executive ranks to change. It

54
00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:07.550
treats lived experience as genuine expertise during program design and

55
00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:08.450
accessibility testing.

56
00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:12.990
And rotating leadership gives more people visible experience that supports

57
00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:13.510
promotion.

58
00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.320
And yet our opening story shows the shadow side of

59
00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.600
that. Leadership that is real but unrecognized, and quietly heavy

60
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:22.720
for the people carrying it.

61
00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:27.590
Exactly. When staff lead without authority, workload relief or recognition,

62
00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:31.490
shared leadership becomes unpaid extra labor, and it often falls

63
00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:34.090
on the same people. There is also a risk that

64
00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:37.830
when equity is everyone's responsibility, it becomes no one's job.

65
00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.170
Without a named owner for accessibility checks or data reviews

66
00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:44.490
in each phase, those standards slip during busy periods.

67
00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.160
So what should the supervisor say to the colleague who

68
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.260
is stretched thin? Thanking her warmly is good, but it

69
00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:52.220
does not seem like enough.

70
00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:57.630
Make the leadership work visible: recognize it, adjust her workload,

71
00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.910
and write it into roles and reviews. Shared leadership lasts

72
00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:04.090
when the work is seen and resourced. Simply taking her

73
00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:06.870
tasks away would relieve her, but it would also take

74
00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.150
away an opportunity she values.

75
00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.760
What else does the page suggest for teams that want

76
00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.840
to share leadership well, and keep it sustainable over time?

77
00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:18.950
Name one lead, one approver and the contributors for every

78
00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:23.850
phase, so accountability never disappears. When someone steps up to

79
00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:27.130
lead, adjust their regular workload and document their leadership in

80
00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:30.770
evaluations and promotion materials. And track who gets to lead

81
00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.230
visible work, so informal circles do not monopolize it and

82
00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:35.990
quieter experts are supported to step forward.

83
00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.760
Here is a question to carry with you. What leadership

84
00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.540
work happens on your team that no one officially owns,

85
00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:45.020
and who is carrying it? Thank you for listening.
